Once you stop casting and start parsing, the whole job is a handful of calls: `parseInt` to read a number out of text, `valueOf` when you want the wrapper object, and `String.valueOf` or `Integer.toString` to go back the other way. The line worth pausing on is the `parseInt` inside a try-catch — that is the shape every conversion of untrusted input should take.

```java
public class StringIntConversion {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int primitive = Integer.parseInt("42");   // String -> int
        Integer wrapper = Integer.valueOf("42");   // String -> Integer (boxed)

        try {
            Integer.parseInt("12abc");            // throws NumberFormatException
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid number");
        }

        int number = 100;
        String s1 = String.valueOf(number);        // preferred for int -> String
        String s2 = Integer.toString(number);      // also good
        int hex = Integer.parseInt("FF", 16);      // 255 — radix 16
        System.out.println(primitive + wrapper + s1 + s2 + hex);
    }
}
```

Notice the last line takes a radix: `parseInt("FF", 16)` reads hex and gives you `255`. That second argument is how you parse binary, octal, or hex without writing your own digit logic — a detail most freshers never realize `parseInt` supports.
